THE FAILING TEST, IN FULL, AND IT IS DEC-89's — because it is the one where the shipped answer is
HTTP 200 and the damage is somebody's itinerary:

```go
// ABSENT MEANS LEAVE ALONE. Executed by the safety lens against a real build of
// HEAD 89fc93f: PUT /v1/trips/autumn with three cities and both dates -> 200 and
// trip_cities reads kyoto:0 / osaka:1 / seoul:2; then a body of {id, name} —
// which is EXACTLY what T4's pencil sends, because renameTrip owns the name and
// nothing else — answers 200 with "cityIds": [], "start": null, "end": null and
// SELECT count(*) FROM trip_cities -> 0.
//
// THE BODY IN THIS LEG IS THE BODY THE CLIENT SENDS. A synthesised whole entity
// cannot fail this way, which is why every leg written against one has been green
// since the route shipped.
func TestRenamingATripLeavesItsItineraryAndItsDatesAlone(t *testing.T) {
	db, travellerID := freshTraveller(t)
	store := postgres.LogbookStore{DB: db}
	seedCities(t, db, travellerID, "kyoto", "osaka", "seoul")

	start, end := logbook.At(day(2027, 9, 18)), logbook.At(day(2027, 10, 2))
	if _, _, err := store.PutTrip(t.Context(), travellerID, logbook.TripWrite{
		ID:      ptr("autumn"),
		Name:    ptr("Autumn crossing"),
		CityIDs: ptr([]string{"kyoto", "osaka", "seoul"}),
		Start:   ptr(&start),
		End:     ptr(&end),
	}); err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("PutTrip: %v", err)
	}

	// T4's pencil. Two keys. Nothing else is in the body at all.
	got, _, err := store.PutTrip(t.Context(), travellerID, logbook.TripWrite{
		ID:   ptr("autumn"),
		Name: ptr("Autumn crossing, renamed"),
	})
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("PutTrip rename: %v", err)
	}

	if len(got.CityIDs) != 3 {
		t.Errorf("cityIds = %v after a name-only PUT, want three — a rename that "+
			"empties the itinerary answers 200 and Trip.phaseAt then reads hasDates, "+
			"so the trip silently leaves its year section on T1 and T2", got.CityIDs)
	}
	if got.Start == nil || got.End == nil {
		t.Errorf("start/end = %v/%v after a name-only PUT, want both intact — "+
			"D3's own subtitle would then read 'No dates yet, 0 cities' about a "+
			"trip that had three", got.Start, got.End)
	}
	if got.Name != "Autumn crossing, renamed" {
		t.Errorf("name = %q, want the new one — the point is that the field that "+
			"WAS sent still gets written", got.Name)
	}
	if n := countTripCities(t, db, travellerID, "autumn"); n != 3 {
		t.Errorf("trip_cities holds %d rows, want 3 — the response and the table "+
			"must agree, and asserting only on the response would pass against a "+
			"handler that echoes its input", n)
	}
}
```

EXPECTED RED, and it is an ASSERTION failure rather than a build failure once the pointers land:
`cityIds = [] after a name-only PUT, want three`. Before the type change it is
`cannot use ptr("autumn") (value of type *string) as string`, which is a build failure and is the
right red for a claim about types (VS6 settled that). RECORD BOTH: the build red proves the contract
changed, and the assertion red proves the STATEMENT changed, and a worker who only makes it compile
has done half of it.

THE SECOND FAILING LEG — DEC-90, and it needs an input no fixture contains:

```go
// The client's ONLY date input is pickLogDate, which is showDatePicker returning
// DateTime(y,m,d) in the DEVICE'S LOCAL ZONE. _addDates passes it straight
// through (grep -rn 'toUtc' lib/src/screens/ returns nothing) and trip.g.dart
// writes toIso8601String(), which emits NO ZONE SUFFIX for a non-UTC value.
// Measured live: PUT with "2027-10-12T00:00:00.000" -> 400 invalid_body; the
// identical body with ...000Z -> 200. So T4's 'Add dates' fails on EVERY request.
//
// THE SERVER KEEPS REFUSING (DEC-90). Guessing at a zoneless string's meaning is
// the class of thing logbookFormatVersion exists to refuse. The CLIENT converts,
// and that is a CLIENT-PREREQUISITES item — and a local-format change as well as
// a wire one, because it changes what logbook.json holds.
func TestAZonelessDateIsRefusedAndNotReinterpreted(t *testing.T) {
	var w logbook.TripWrite
	err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(`{"id":"autumn","start":"2027-10-12T00:00:00.000"}`), &w)
	if err == nil {
		t.Fatalf("a zoneless date decoded to %v — the server must refuse it rather "+
			"than assume a zone; a silent reinterpretation is a date that means one "+
			"thing on the phone and another in the log", w.Start)
	}
	if !strings.Contains(err.Error(), "RFC 3339") {
		t.Errorf("error = %q, want it to name RFC 3339 — the message is what tells "+
			"a client developer to call .toUtc()", err)
	}

	// AND THE PRECISION HALF, which the fixture also cannot supply. instantLayout
	// is .000 and Go TRUNCATES; Dart writes SIX digits whenever microsecond != 0,
	// which logbookNow() always has on the VM. Photo.filedLater and a minted
	// Visit.at can both carry six.
	var six logbook.TripWrite
	if err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(`{"id":"a","start":"2027-10-12T00:00:00.123456Z"}`), &six); err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("a six-digit fraction was refused: %v — millisecond precision is "+
			"the contract and the client truncates before sending, so this must "+
			"decode", err)
	}
	if got := six.Start.Format(logbook.InstantLayout); got != "2027-10-12T00:00:00.123Z" {
		t.Errorf("re-emitted as %q, want the truncation stated as the contract", got)
	}
}
```

THE THIRD FAILING LEG — DEC-91, and v7.1's version of it passes because it uses a trip with no link:

```go
// DEC-32's write response is a whole Trip the phone SPLICES into its cached log.
// With DEC-85 hashing tokens at rest that Trip carries shareLinkId: null, and the
// CLIENT HOLDS THE ONLY COPY. So an ordinary rename overwrites it, H1 renders no
// URL, and both 'Copy link' and 'Stop sharing' go inert — while the row is
// un-revoked and GET /l/{token} still serves it. Trip.withName's own doc states
// the invariant verbatim: 'renaming a trip you have shared must not quietly kill
// the URL somebody is holding.'
//
// THE PLAN'S EXISTING GUARD IS BLIND TO IT: 'the write's answer spliced into the
// cached document decodes equal to the next whole read' — and the next whole read
// is null too. THIS LEG USES A TRIP THAT HAS A LIVE LINK. That is the difference.
func TestARenamedTripStillReportsThatItIsShared(t *testing.T) {
	db, travellerID := freshTraveller(t)
	store := postgres.LogbookStore{DB: db}
	putTrip(t, store, travellerID, "autumn", "Autumn crossing")
	insertLiveShareLink(t, db, travellerID, "autumn", "kyoto-9f2a") // un-revoked

	got, _, err := store.PutTrip(t.Context(), travellerID, logbook.TripWrite{
		ID: ptr("autumn"), Name: ptr("Autumn crossing, renamed"),
	})
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if !got.Shared {
		t.Errorf("shared = false on a trip whose share_links row is un-revoked — "+
			"the phone splices this answer over its cache, so H1 loses the URL AND "+
			"the only control that revokes it, from an action that has nothing to "+
			"do with sharing")
	}

	revokeShareLink(t, db, travellerID, "autumn")
	after, _, _ := store.PutTrip(t.Context(), travellerID, logbook.TripWrite{
		ID: ptr("autumn"), Name: ptr("Autumn crossing")})
	if after.Shared {
		t.Errorf("shared = true after revocation — the field must be DERIVED from "+
			"share_links and not stored, or it is a second place for the same fact")
	}
}
```

THE FOURTH — DEC-94, and it is the one where the server reports success:

```go
// GET /v1/logbook answers 200 with a VALID ETag and a body cut mid-token when
// body/link-speed exceeds WriteTimeout. Reproduced three times by the performance
// lens against an 11,102,597-byte log: at 400 kB/s, 8,371,312 bytes received,
// curl exit 18, json.load -> 'Unterminated string starting at char 8371258', last
// bytes on disk {"lat":35.0409862745072,"lng. At 500 kB/s: 9,631,256 bytes, code
// 200. At 200 kB/s: 5,622,648 bytes, code 200.
//
// COMPRESSION DOES NOT REMOVE THE CLASS, IT MOVES THE THRESHOLD 5-15x. Both
// halves are here because either alone is a half-measure, and the Vary header is
// not hygiene: without it the next cache in front of this serves compressed bytes
// to a client that did not ask.
func TestTheWholeLogIsCompressedWhenAskedAndSaysSo(t *testing.T) {
	srv := newAPI(t)
	token := registerAndSignIn(t, srv)

	plain := getWith(t, srv, token, "/v1/logbook", nil)
	gz := getWith(t, srv, token, "/v1/logbook", http.Header{"Accept-Encoding": {"gzip"}})

	if gz.Header.Get("Content-Encoding") != "gzip" {
		t.Fatalf("Content-Encoding = %q, want gzip", gz.Header.Get("Content-Encoding"))
	}
	if !strings.Contains(gz.Header.Get("Vary"), "Accept-Encoding") {
		t.Errorf("Vary = %q, want it to name Accept-Encoding — an intermediary that "+
			"does not see this serves gzip to a client that did not ask for it",
			gz.Header.Get("Vary"))
	}
	if plain.Header.Get("Content-Encoding") != "" {
		t.Errorf("a client that did not ask got %q", plain.Header.Get("Content-Encoding"))
	}
	// The ETag is a fact about the LOG and not about the encoding.
	if gz.Header.Get("Etag") != plain.Header.Get("Etag") {
		t.Errorf("etags differ by encoding: %q vs %q — a client that switches "+
			"Accept-Encoding would then re-download an unchanged log",
			gz.Header.Get("Etag"), plain.Header.Get("Etag"))
	}
	if !bytes.Equal(gunzip(t, gz.Body), plain.Body) {
		t.Error("the compressed body does not decompress to the plain one")
	}
}
```

THE FIFTH — DEC-96, and it must be written so that its only failure mode is not a timeout:

```go
// R1's lock_timeout bounds the MIGRATION'S WAIT and nothing else (OE-19). The
// operations lens executed what that leaves: with an ALTER queued behind an open
// reader, ONE GET /v1/logbook returned curl http=000 at its own 30s limit — no
// status, no body, no error — and ten concurrent ones all returned 000 after
// 18.8s, with 9 backends active/Lock. /healthz answered 200 in 4.7ms throughout,
// because it pings and never touches trips, and docker said healthy.
//
// ASSERT ON THE ANSWER, NOT ON THE ELAPSED TIME. A leg whose only failure mode is
// a hang is a leg somebody eventually deletes, so this one answers from a
// goroutine behind a select with a deadline and asserts the STATUS.
func TestARequestBlockedOnALockGetsABoundedAnswerAndNotSilence(t *testing.T) {
	srv, db := newAPIWithDB(t)
	token := registerAndSignIn(t, srv)

	release := holdAccessExclusive(t, db, "trips") // a real lock, held
	defer release()

	type answer struct {
		status int
		retry  string
	}
	ch := make(chan answer, 1)
	go func() {
		r := getWith(t, srv, token, "/v1/logbook", nil)
		ch <- answer{r.StatusCode, r.Header.Get("Retry-After")}
	}()

	select {
	case got := <-ch:
		if got.status != 503 {
			t.Errorf("status = %d, want 503 — a request that cannot reach the "+
				"database has not encountered a handler bug, and 500 tells a "+
				"client not to retry", got.status)
		}
		if got.retry == "" {
			t.Errorf("no Retry-After — 'the dependency is down, try again shortly' "+
				"is the whole difference between this and a poison request")
		}
	case <-time.After(2 * config.RequestTimeout):
		t.Fatal("no answer at all within twice REQUEST_TIMEOUT — this is the " +
			"measured shape: curl http=000, nothing logged, /healthz green")
	}
}
```

