MUTATIONS, and each names the OTHER leg that must stay green, because 'this reddens' is satisfiable
by a mutation that breaks everything:
  * Make `TripWrite.CityIDs` a bare `[]string` again — leg one reddens on `cityIds` and leg six does
    NOT, which is the pair proving the pointer contract and the share defaults are two mechanisms.
  * Accept a zoneless date by falling back to a layout without a zone — leg two reddens and nothing
    else does. This is the mutation that would have shipped: it is the CHEAP fix the lens itself
    lists as option (b), and it is refused.
  * Store `shared` as a column instead of deriving it — leg three's SECOND half reddens (the
    revocation), and its first half stays green. That asymmetry is the whole argument for the
    subquery.
  * Drop `Vary` — leg four reddens on Vary only; the Content-Encoding assertions stay green.
  * Remove the 503 branch and let the driver error become a 500 — leg five reddens on the status and
    NOT on the deadline, which is what distinguishes 'answers wrongly' from 'does not answer'.
  * Delete the `UPDATE` from 0002 — leg seven reddens and leg six does NOT.
  * Revert 0002's `true` to `false` for `share_notes` ONLY — leg six reddens naming shareNotes and
    not sharePhotos, which proves the two assertions are independent rather than one written twice.
  * Log `durationMs` again — leg ten reddens on the non-zero assertion and not on the presence one.
  * Remove `lock_timeout` — leg nine hangs, so it is written to answer from a goroutine behind a
    `select` with a deadline.

TEST-FIRST, at TWO SEAMS, and which is which is stated rather than blended (DEC-16).

Seam 1 — handler-level, against media.Memory, in `go test ./...` with no daemon.
Seam 2 — INTEGRATION against real MinIO at a PINNED image tag, gated on TEST_S3_ENDPOINT with an ASSERTED SKIP MESSAGE, exactly as the database tier is. A skipped leg that does not say it skipped is a green that means nothing.

The failing test, in full, written and watched to fail before internal/media exists. It is the checksum leg because that is the one the whole step exists for:

```go
//go:build integration

// THE UPLOAD MUST BE REFUSED BY THE BUCKET, NOT BY US. A presigned PUT whose
// signature does not cover the client's digest lets a lying client write
// arbitrary bytes at an address that claims to be their hash — and every
// later reader trusts the address. minio-go's PresignedPutObject and Presign
// both produce exactly that URL and both look perfectly correct until
// somebody uploads the wrong bytes, which is why they are banned in
// internal/media/minio.go and why this leg is written against a REAL server:
// MinIO is a forgiving verifier, and it is still the strictest one we have
// short of S3 itself.
func TestABodyThatDoesNotMatchThePresignedDigestIsRefusedByTheBucket(t *testing.T) {
	store := realMinIO(t) // skips with a named message when TEST_S3_ENDPOINT is unset

	honest := []byte("the bytes the client promised")
	sum := sha256.Sum256(honest)
	digest := hex.EncodeToString(sum[:])

	url, err := store.PresignPut(t.Context(), media.Key{Traveller: travellerID, Object: digest},
		media.Upload{SHA256: digest, ByteSize: int64(len(honest)), ContentType: "image/png"})
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("PresignPut: %v", err)
	}

	// THE HEADERS MUST BE REPLAYED OR THIS LEG CANNOT SEE THE CHECKSUM AT ALL.
	// v7.1's version set none, and MinIO answered 400 AccessDenied ("There were
	// headers present in the request which were not signed") for LYING bytes AND
	// for HONEST bytes alike — so the leg was GREEN with zero digest verification
	// happening anywhere. FOUR failure modes land in the 4xx class and only the
	// last is the control: AccessDenied (header not replayed), InvalidArgument
	// (hex where base64 belongs), SignatureDoesNotMatch (wrong signed-header set),
	// XAmzContentChecksumMismatch (the actual thing). Assert the CODE.
	lying := []byte("entirely different bytes of the same length!!")
	req, _ := http.NewRequestWithContext(t.Context(), http.MethodPut, url, bytes.NewReader(lying))
	for k, v := range headers { // exactly what PresignPut returned; DEC-88's map
		req.Header.Set(k, v)
	}
	resp, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("PUT: %v", err)
	}
	defer resp.Body.Close()

	if code := s3ErrorCode(t, resp); code != "XAmzContentChecksumMismatch" {
		t.Fatalf("the bucket answered %d %s for bytes that are not the digest the URL was "+
			"signed for; want XAmzContentChecksumMismatch. AccessDenied here means the "+
			"leg did not replay a signed header and is measuring nothing; "+
			"InvalidArgument means the digest was signed as hex rather than base64",
			resp.StatusCode, code)
	}

	// And the object must not be there at all: a refused PUT that still wrote
	// something is the same defect wearing a 4xx.
	if _, err := store.Stat(t.Context(), media.Key{Traveller: travellerID, Object: digest}); err == nil {
		t.Fatal("the object exists after a refused upload")
	}
}
```

EXPECTED RED, and the right reason is `undefined: media.Store` — a build failure, which VS6 settled is the right red for a claim about types. It becomes an ASSERTION failure the moment a naive implementation exists, and THAT is the run to record: implement PresignPut with `PresignedPutObject` first, watch this leg fail with the bucket returning 200, and only then sign the checksum. Recording that intermediate red is what proves the leg can see the defect it was written for; a leg written after the correct implementation cannot say so.

SECOND FAILING LEG, NEW AT v7.1 (PD-11) — THE LENGTH, and it is the checksum leg's twin because the security lens found the two halves were not twins in v7.0:

```go
//go:build integration

// DEC-51 asked for content-length SIGNED INTO the PUT 'so the BUCKET enforces
// it rather than the API hoping'. An API-side 422 before signing does not bind
// the holder of the URL — the 422 happens before the capability is minted —
// and a HEAD on commit only refuses to MARK the object committed: the bytes
// are already in the bucket, and the sweep that would reclaim them is out of
// scope for all eight steps.
func TestABodyLongerThanTheSignedLengthIsRefusedByTheBucket(t *testing.T) {
	store := realMinIO(t)

	honest := []byte("the bytes the client promised")
	sum := sha256.Sum256(honest)
	digest := hex.EncodeToString(sum[:])

	url, err := store.PresignPut(t.Context(), media.Key{Traveller: travellerID, Object: digest},
		media.Upload{SHA256: digest, ByteSize: int64(len(honest)), ContentType: "image/png"})
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("PresignPut: %v", err)
	}

	oversized := bytes.Repeat([]byte("x"), len(honest)*1000)
	req, _ := http.NewRequestWithContext(t.Context(), http.MethodPut, url, bytes.NewReader(oversized))
	resp, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("PUT: %v", err)
	}
	defer resp.Body.Close()

	if resp.StatusCode < 400 {
		t.Fatalf("the bucket accepted %d bytes against a URL signed for %d: status %d — "+
			"one legitimately-minted upload URL is then an unbounded write that "+
			"nothing in these eight steps reclaims", len(oversized), len(honest), resp.StatusCode)
	}
	if _, err := store.Stat(t.Context(), media.Key{Traveller: travellerID, Object: digest}); err == nil {
		t.Fatal("the object exists after a refused upload")
	}
}
```

OTHER LEGS: presign a PUT, upload the honest bytes, HEAD, presign a GET, fetch back, bytes match. **THE TTL LEG IS REPLACED, NOT KEPT** (OE-15): v7.0 asserted the two TTLs are distinct and both come from config, which cannot fail in the way that matters — a handler reaching for the PRIVATE lifetime where the public one belongs would redden nothing. The replacement asserts WHICH TTL each call site uses, with a mutation swapping them. The config half is kept, because a TTL that is not configurable closes DEC-21's tuning question by accident. The key function is one place, and a leg asserts one traveller's key can never address another's object — because the bucket is keyed the same way the table is, and the day a second traveller registers a sweep that got this wrong deletes somebody else's photographs.

MUTATIONS: drop the length from the signature (the new leg reddens and the checksum leg does NOT — which is the pair that proves they are two controls rather than one); swap PresignPut's signing for PresignedPutObject (the checksum leg reddens against real MinIO and against nothing else); point the key function at `<sha256>` without the traveller prefix (the cross-traveller leg reddens); use the private TTL where the public one belongs (a leg comparing the two reddens — and this is the mutation DEC-47 exists for).

THE POSITIVE CONTROL, IN THE SAME FUNCTION, AND WITHOUT IT THE LEG IS SATISFIED BY A SERVER THAT REFUSES EVERYTHING (PD-19, STO-BLO-1, STO-MIN-10). Honest bytes plus the same headers -> 200, `Stat` returns the expected size, and `StatObject` with `Checksum: true` returns the digest the address claims. It is the same shape v6 already required of the committed-asset validator and the same shape R3's begin/commit leg has: a negative half and a positive half in one leg, because the mutation that proves the negative is not vacuous is 'make it reject everything'.

THE WRITE-ONCE LEG (DEC-88): PUT the honest bytes -> 200; PUT the SAME bytes again -> 412 PreconditionFailed; PUT DIFFERENT bytes at the same key with a self-consistent checksum for those bytes -> 412, and `Stat` afterwards returns the ORIGINAL size. That third assertion is the attack, and it is the one a size check alone cannot see.

THE TTL LEG DEC-84 MANDATES, WHICH IS THE REPLACEMENT OE-15 PROMISED: assert WHICH TTL EACH CALL SITE USES, by inspecting the `X-Amz-Expires` the signer produced — the PRIVATE value from the mint path and the PUBLIC 15m from the envelope path — with a mutation that swaps them. The config half is kept, because a TTL that is not configurable closes DEC-21's tuning question by accident.

THE BUCKET LEG (DEC-98): against a MinIO with a fresh volume and NO bucket, boot the store and assert `BucketExists` is true afterwards; then assert `EnsureBucket` a second time is a no-op and not an error. The mutation is to delete the call from `main.go` — the round-trip leg reddens and `up --wait` still reports three healthy services, which is the whole point.

AND ONE LEG THAT MUST NOT BE WRITTEN, recorded so that it is not written by somebody who curls MinIO and sees a header (STO-MIN-11): DO NOT assert `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` on a presigned GET. MinIO sets it of its own accord, so the leg would pass locally by measuring MinIO's default and would silently stop being true against real S3, whose response-header override set is closed and cannot produce it. That is DEC-43's 'MinIO is a more forgiving verifier' hazard running in the OPPOSITE direction, where the local server is more generous than the real one. V4-SF3's reasoning stays as a comment.

ADDITIONAL MUTATIONS at v7.2: omit one signed header from the replay in the checksum leg (the leg reddens with `AccessDenied`, which is the RIGHT red for the WRONG reason and is exactly why the assertion is on the code); sign the digest as hex rather than base64 (the leg reddens with `InvalidArgument`, distinctly); derive the key and the header from two variables rather than one function (the write-once leg reddens); drop `If-None-Match` from the signature (the second PUT answers 200 and the third assertion — the original size — reddens); drop `EnsureBucket` from main.go (the round-trip leg reddens and `up --wait` does not).

